{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Yosef dreamed, etc.</b> One needs to know why Yosef would increase the hatred by telling them his dreams of greatness, especially since he knew that he was hated in their eyes — as they said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 84:11), on the verse ״וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ הִנֵּנִי״, that he surrendered himself to death. But perhaps it is because he intended to inform them that from Heaven they had elevated him on high, and the act of Yaakov was with the agreement of the Supernal Ones, and from this they would desist from hating him." "<b>Or</b> he may have intended to inform them that they would still need him and would come and bow down to him. Or perhaps it is because by their knowing thus, they would distance the hatred, so that he would not take vengeance against them at the time they would fall before him — for the tribes would bow their heads to the decree of El Elyon and to His will." "<b>Or</b> he may have intended to draw the hearts closer together, and this is along the lines of what they said in the chapter HaRo'eh (Berakhot 55b), that all dreams follow the mouth. And they further said that one should go to one who loves him (Shabbat, same passage) and he will interpret it for him, and Yosef thought that by telling them his dreams, they would judge on their own that his heart was whole with them, according to the way of beloved brothers. But since hatred and jealousy had preceded, the gates of love were locked within their hearts, and they judged everything as an act of lording it over them, etc." "<b>And Yosef dreamed, etc.</b> Scripture testifies on his behalf that he dreamed, to remove the suspicion that he was inventing from his own heart that he had dreamed, in order to lord it over them. Also in the second dream Scripture informed (Genesis 37:9): ״וַיַּחֲלֹם״ — to vindicate Yosef's words." "<b>And he told his brothers.</b> That is, he dreamed a good dream, which in their eyes was as hard as sinews. And Scripture did not mention the dream itself, for it is what he said immediately after: ״שִׁמְעוּ נָא״. And one cannot say that it was a different dream, for if so, why did Scripture not inform us of it? And if the reason is that it was not fulfilled, why would Scripture tell of a vain dream? And if the intention is to inform us of the cause of the hatred — behold, the preceding dream and the two dreams would suffice. Rather, it is certainly the dream that he spoke of immediately after. And at first he told them only vaguely: a dream of high standing I dreamed; and since they did not wish to hear his dream because of their hatred, he broke off the telling and entreated them to listen to him, and said: ״שִׁמְעוּ נָא״, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:28.287810636Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}